Context:
We have an IBM Db2 11.5.9 LUW database running HADR with one existing principal standby in NEARSYNC + ACR (Peer state). Reads on Standby is not available. We want to add a temporary second standby node, bring it to Peer state, detach it from HADR, and create a reusable offline "golden" backup for repeated testing — without impacting the primary or the existing standby at any point.

Goal:
Provide the complete step-by-step command sequence to:
1. Update hadr_target_list on the Primary and existing Standby 1 to include the new Standby 2 node
2. Take an online backup from the Primary
3. Restore it on Standby 2 (leaving it in rollforward pending)
4. Configure HADR parameters on Standby 2 (using SUPERASYNC sync mode, as required for auxiliary standbys)
5. Start HADR on Standby 2 and bring it to Peer state
6. Stop HADR on Standby 2
7. Roll forward Standby 2 to end of logs and stop, making it a standalone database
8. Clear all HADR config parameters on Standby 2
9. Take a full offline backup of Standby 2 as a reusable golden backup
10. Revert hadr_target_list on Primary and Standby 1 back to single-entry

For each command, specify:
- Which node it runs on (Primary, Standby 1, or Standby 2)
- Whether a DB/instance restart is required

Constraints:
- No downtime on Primary or Standby 1 at any point
- Standby 2 is temporary — it will be dropped and restored from the golden backup for each test run
- The golden backup must restore as a fully standalone database with no HADR config and no rollforward required
- Archive logs are stored on a shared NAS accessible to all nodes

The golden backup will be reused across multiple independent test scenarios, including:
1. Offline and online REORG testing — to measure duration and assess maintenance window requirements
2. Db2 version upgrade testing — restoring the golden backup on a fresh Db2 12 instance to validate
   upgrade procedures, compatibility, and post-upgrade behaviour
3. OS upgrade testing — restoring the golden backup on a RHEL 9 host to validate that the database
   operates correctly on the new OS before committing to a production upgrade path

Placeholders to substitute with actual values:
- <DB> = database name
- <PRIMARY_HOST> = primary hostname
- <PRIMARY_SVC> = primary HADR service port
- <PRIMARY_INST> = primary instance name
- <STANDBY1_HOST> = existing standby hostname
- <STANDBY1_SVC> = existing standby HADR service port
- <STANDBY2_HOST> = new temporary standby hostname
- <STANDBY2_SVC> = new temporary standby HADR service port
- <NAS_BACKUP_PATH> = NAS path for backups
- <NAS_ARCHIVE_PATH> = NAS path for archive logs
